G e n e r a l   A l g e b r a i c   M o d e l i n g   S y s t e m
C o m p i l a t i o n


      CEE 6410
      Example 6 in the Lecture notes.
       
      Minimize cost to supply water by a new treatment plant or wholesale contract
      This is the relaxed version that treats the interger decision variables as standard, positive, continuous variables.
      With the additional $35,000 capital cost for establishing the wholesale contract.
       
      Allows user to control bounds on "integer" variables (IntUpBnd and IntLowBnd).
       
      Presently fully relaxed.
       
      David E Rosenberg
      david.rosenberg@usu.edu
      September 24, 2020
  17   
  18  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  19  SETS src water supply sources /tp "treatment plant", wc "wholesale contract"/;
  20   
  21  * 2. DEFINE input data
  22  PARAMETERS
  23     CapCost(src) capital cost ($ to build)
  24           /tp 90000,
  25            wc 35000/
  26     OpCost(src) operating cost ($ per ac-ft)
  27           /tp 120
  28            wc 150/
  29     MaxCapacity(src) Maximum capacity of source when built (ac-ft per year)
  30            /tp 2000,
  31             wc  12000/
  32     MinUse(src) Minimum required use of source when built (ac-ft per year)
  33            /tp 0,
  34             wc  1000/
  35     TotDemand  Total Demand (ac-ft per year) /2000/
  36   
  37   
  38  * "Integer" variables free within 0 to 1 bounds.
  39  * Allows user to relax, tighten bounds on integer variables
  40  * Presently set in fully relaxed mode
  41     IntUpBnd(src) Upper bound on integer variables (#)
  42            /tp 1,
  43             wc 1 /
  44   
  45  IntLowBnd(src) Lower bound on integer variables (#)
  46             /tp 0,
  47             wc 1/ ;
  48   
  49  * 3. DEFINE the decision variables
  50  VARIABLES I(src) binary decision to build or do prject from source src (1=yes 0=no)
  51            X(src) volume of water provided by source src (ac-ft per year)
  52            TCOST  total capital and operating costs of supply actions ($);
  53   
  54  * Non-negativity constraints
  55  POSITIVE VARIABLES X;
  56   
  57  *Binary Variables I ;
  58   
  59  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  60  EQUATIONS
  61     COST            Total Cost ($) and objective function value
  62     MaxCap(src)     Maximum capacity of source when built (ac-ft per year)
  63     MinReqUse(src)  Minimum required use of source when built (ac-ft per year)
  64     MeetDemand      Meet demand (ac-ft per year)
  65     IntUpBound(src) Upper bound on interger variables (number)
  66     IntLowBound(src) Lower bound on integer variables (number);
  67   
  68  COST..                 TCOST =E= SUM(src,CapCost(src)*I(src) + OpCost(src)*X(src));
  69  MaxCap(src) ..           X(src) =L= MaxCapacity(src)*I(src);
  70  MinReqUse(src) ..        X(src) =G= MinUse(src)*I(src);
  71  MeetDemand ..            sum(src,X(src)) =G= TotDemand;
  72   
  73  * for bounds
  74  IntUpBound(src) ..       I(src) =L= IntUpBnd(src);
  75  IntLowBound(src) ..      I(src) =G= IntLowBnd(src);
  76   
  77  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
  78  MODEL WatSupplyRelaxed /ALL/;
  79   
  80  * 6. Solve the Model as an LP (relaxed IP)
  81  SOLVE WatSupplyRelaxed USING MIP MINIMIZING TCOST;
  82   
  83  DISPLAY X.L, I.L, TCOST.L;
  84   
      * Dump all input data and results to a GAMS gdx file
      Execute_Unload "Ex6-3-relaxed.gdx";
      * Dump the gdx file to an Excel workbook
      Execute "gdx2xls Ex6-3.relaxed.gdx"


